          }
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Finishing" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fasten off, leaving a 15 cm tail. Weave through 3 to 4 stitches on the wrong side and trim. Weave in the magic ring tail. Steam-block on a foam mat: pin all six points to keep the hexagon symmetrical, then pin the six sides straight between the points."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Joining" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hexagons join flat along each of their six sides. For a honeycomb grid, each hexagon shares one full side with its neighbour — whip-stitch through the back loops along the side clusters and through the adjacent corner ch-2 spaces. A join-as-you-go method works on round 3: replace the ch-2 at each shared corner with a ch-1, sl st into the corresponding corner sp of the adjacent hexagon, ch-1, then continue."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Care" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash in cool water with mild soap, or machine-wash on a delicates cycle. Lay flat to dry — hexagons hold their shape better if pinned at the six points while damp."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "What to try next" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The granny-style hexagon uses the same six-corner construction but adds side clusters in the later rounds to match the aesthetic of a granny square — useful when mixing hexagons and squares in the same project. The treble cluster hexagon replaces the open ch-2 corner with a bold 5-tr cluster at each point."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
